<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hou Jing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">503–<a href="/years/552">552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hou Jing was a general of Eastern Wei who defected to the southern Liang dynasty after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> in 547. In 548 he rebelled, besieged the Liang capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and took it in 549, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Emperor Wu of Liang</a> died in his hands. He proclaimed himself emperor of Han in 551 but was defeated by Liang forces under Wang Sengbian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-chen">Chen Baxian</a> and killed in 552. His rebellion devastated southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hou-jing.jpg" alt="Hou Jing" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hou_Jing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hou Jing, courtesy name Wanjing, came from Huaishuo Garrison, one of the Six Garrisons that Northern Wei kept on its northern border against the Rouran. He appears to have been ethnically Chinese, though the point is not settled: his enemies called him a Jie, which led many modern scholars to place him among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jie-people">Jie people</a>, while some historians note that the word was a common insult of the time. He probably took on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> ways among the garrisons, and as a young man he belonged to a circle of friends around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> he became known as a gifted tactician, although he was no great horseman or archer and walked with a limp. In late 534 he defeated Heba Sheng and won the southern provinces for Eastern Wei, and from 536 he commanded the provinces south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. He had little regard for Gao Huan's heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-cheng">Gao Cheng</a>, whom he called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> boy. In spring 547 he rebelled, surrendering his 13 provinces first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> and then to Liang; when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> demanded that he come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> to pay homage, he refused.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Emperor Wu of Liang</a> welcomed him, but when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-cheng">Gao Cheng</a> offered peace, Hou forged a letter from Gao proposing to exchange Xiao Yuanming, a nephew of the emperor held by Eastern Wei, for himself. The emperor's reply, that Xiao Yuanming returned in the morning would mean Hou delivered in the evening, decided him on rebellion, and he gained the secret support of the emperor's nephew Xiao Zhengde, the Prince of Linhe. The years from 548 to 552 are known as the Hou Jing Disturbance. As emperor of Han he took the era name Taishi, and he put captured opponents to death with great cruelty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-liang">Xiao Yi</a>'s generals Wang Sengbian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-chen">Chen Baxian</a>, Hou fled, killing his two young sons born at Jiankang. He made for the sea, but while he slept his guard Yang Kun turned the boats towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkou">Jingkou</a> and killed him with a spear. His body was salted and sent to Jiankang, his head to Xiao Yi and his hands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a>, and the crowd, among them his wife Princess Liyang, cut off his flesh and ate it. Hou Jing is one of the most reviled figures in imperial Chinese history; about five years after his death Chen Baxian overthrew Liang and founded the Chen dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hou Jing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Liang dynasty collapsed in the 550s under the <b>Hou Jing</b> rebellion and northern invasions.</blockquote><a href="/state/chen-dynasty">Chen dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>After Gao Huan's death in 547 his general <b>Hou Jing</b> defected to the south, and his son Gao Cheng was assassinated in 549.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-wei">Eastern Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>In 548 the defecting general <b>Hou Jing</b> rebelled and besieged Jiankang; Emperor Wu died a captive in 549.</blockquote><a href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
